Let $P(x) = (x^2-4x+3)(x+5) - (x^2+4x-5)(x-c)$. We want to find the constant $c$ such that $P(x) = 0$ for all $x$.
Expanding the terms, we have:
\begin{align*} P(x) &= (x^3 - 4x^2 + 3x + 5x^2 - 20x + 15) - (x^3 + 4x^2 - 5x - cx^2 - 4cx + 5c) \\ &= (x^3 + x^2 - 17x + 15) - (x^3 + (4-c)x^2 + (-5-4c)x + 5c) \\ &= x^3 + x^2 - 17x + 15 - x^3 - (4-c)x^2 - (-5-4c)x - 5c \\ &= x^3 + x^2 - 17x + 15 - x^3 - 4x^2 + cx^2 + 5x + 4cx - 5c \\ &= (1-4+c)x^2 + (-17+5+4c)x + (15-5c) \\ &= (c-3)x^2 + (4c-12)x + (15-5c)\end{aligned}
Since $P(x)=0$ for all $x$, the coefficients of the quadratic must be zero. Thus, we have the following system of equations:
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} c-3 &= 0 \\ 4c-12 &= 0 \\ 15-5c &= 0 \end{align*}

